1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,839
Start in the Chicago Fire demonstration
department.

2
00:00:03,190 --> 00:00:06,820
Engine 51 and our officer personnel are
fictional resources

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:08,220
for a training exercise.

4
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:12,789
Open Dispatch Settings and find Run Cards
and Automatic Dispatch.

5
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:17,079
Select Closest Unit Response for this
example, and leave automatic

6
00:00:17,079 --> 00:00:20,889
dispatch off so a dispatcher can review
the suggested resources.

7
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,409
Save, then reopen the page to confirm the
choices.

8
00:00:24,799 --> 00:00:28,450
A run card can override these department
defaults, so check both

9
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:30,270
places when a result surprises you.

10
00:00:30,519 --> 00:00:32,759
Open Run Cards and choose a new card.

11
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:37,389
Name it Academy Training Alarm, and
describe the exercise it supports.

12
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:41,150
In General, choose Pre-populate for
dispatcher review.

13
00:00:41,530 --> 00:00:43,400
Leave the card disabled while building it.

14
00:00:43,929 --> 00:00:47,459
Home station supplies an origin when a
call has no location;

15
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:50,770
it is not a substitute for checking an
incident address.

16
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:55,299
Notice the other tabs: Triggers, Alarm
Levels, Dispatchable

17
00:00:55,299 --> 00:00:57,549
Statuses, and Test or Simulate.

18
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,469
Each controls a different part of the
recommendation.

19
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:05,120
On Triggers, choose Add Trigger and select
Priority and Type.

20
00:01:05,589 --> 00:01:09,619
Pick the department's training call type
and an actual priority from the list.

21
00:01:09,890 --> 00:01:12,509
This is more specific than matching either
one alone.

22
00:01:13,119 --> 00:01:16,680
On Alarm Levels, name level one Initial
Response.

23
00:01:17,039 --> 00:01:20,769
Add one Engine under Unit type
requirements and one Officer

24
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:22,750
under Personnel role requirements.

25
00:01:23,189 --> 00:01:27,330
Counts describe what the plan asks for;
they do not create resources

26
00:01:27,330 --> 00:01:30,789
or guarantee that a qualified, available
person can be found.

27
00:01:31,189 --> 00:01:33,910
Save this complete card while it remains
disabled.

28
00:01:34,199 --> 00:01:37,229
Open Dispatchable Statuses before widening
the plan.

29
00:01:37,779 --> 00:01:40,960
An empty section follows the standard
availability rules.

30
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:45,599
Selecting options replaces those rules for
that section, so ticking only

31
00:01:45,599 --> 00:01:47,729
one status can exclude the others.

32
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:51,799
To add Returning, begin with Start from
the standard rules,

33
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:53,619
then add the appropriate meaning.

34
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:57,680
Prefer meanings when several custom labels
share the same purpose.

35
00:01:58,170 --> 00:02:01,719
A unit type can have its own rule, so
inspect that section

36
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:03,459
as well as All unit types.

37
00:02:03,629 --> 00:02:07,550
For a reusable response, open Response
packages and create

38
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:09,350
Academy Initial Response.

39
00:02:09,779 --> 00:02:13,900
Add a row that can pick one Engine and
another that can pick one Officer.

40
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:17,219
Any of these allows alternative targets
within a row.

41
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:21,499
Pick fills a count; Ensure first-arriving
adds the earliest

42
00:02:21,499 --> 00:02:24,950
matching resource only when another row
has not already covered it.

43
00:02:25,339 --> 00:02:27,899
Status narrows eligible resources.

44
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:32,370
Keep outstanding until sent preserves an
unmet request for follow-up.

45
00:02:32,749 --> 00:02:35,579
Save and reopen the package to verify the
rows.

46
00:02:35,799 --> 00:02:40,399
Return to Alarm Levels and add a condition
named Engine within eight minutes.

47
00:02:40,799 --> 00:02:46,449
Choose Available within, Engine, a count
of one, and 480 seconds.

48
00:02:46,799 --> 00:02:48,209
Create two outcomes.

49
00:02:48,549 --> 00:02:52,749
The Yes outcome sends Academy Initial
Response; the No outcome

50
00:02:52,749 --> 00:02:56,639
has no package and advises the dispatcher
to review alternatives.

51
00:02:57,020 --> 00:03:02,460
Mark the condition Start, then connect If
yes and If no to the correct outcomes.

52
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:04,729
Every branch needs a destination.

53
00:03:05,030 --> 00:03:08,190
Save the card before testing the decision
you just changed.

54
00:03:08,359 --> 00:03:12,040
Arrives before compares one resource
choice with another,

55
00:03:12,270 --> 00:03:14,200
including turnout and travel time.

56
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:18,019
Use it when an earlier arrival changes the
response you want.

57
00:03:18,549 --> 00:03:22,920
Package rows can name a substitute
package, and the card can relax status

58
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:24,760
filters for rows it cannot fill.

59
00:03:25,199 --> 00:03:28,830
These choices can broaden a response, so
document their purpose.

60
00:03:29,369 --> 00:03:32,600
Missing or stale positions can make a
decision uncertain.

61
00:03:33,039 --> 00:03:37,039
Treat that warning as a reason to review
available evidence, rather than as

62
00:03:37,039 --> 00:03:39,529
proof that no suitable resource exists.

63
00:03:40,070 --> 00:03:44,099
Watch the test with both fixed
requirements and the same package: their

64
00:03:44,099 --> 00:03:48,700
rows add together, so the card asks for
another engine and reports a shortfall.

65
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:53,439
In this example we remove the fixed rows
when moving to the conditional package.

66
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:57,989
The next test fills one engine and one
officer without that duplication.

67
00:03:58,210 --> 00:04:00,000
Open Test or Simulate.

68
00:04:00,459 --> 00:04:03,750
Choose the priority, type, training
coordinates, and

69
00:04:03,779 --> 00:04:05,949
alarm level, then run the test.

70
00:04:06,410 --> 00:04:09,170
Read the matched card and the path through
its conditions.

71
00:04:09,599 --> 00:04:12,580
Check the chosen outcome, recommended
people and units,

72
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:14,739
and any shortfall or uncertainty.

73
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:18,569
This is a preview of current data, not a
dispatched incident.

74
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:21,409
A saved card can be tested while disabled.

75
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:25,119
If you edit a rule, save it and run the
test again so your

76
00:04:25,119 --> 00:04:27,020
review matches the stored plan.

77
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:31,209
At the nearby training coordinates, Engine
51 meets the eight-minute

78
00:04:31,209 --> 00:04:35,349
condition and the Initial Response package
selects an engine and an officer.

79
00:04:35,770 --> 00:04:39,049
At the farther test location, the No
branch shows Review

80
00:04:39,049 --> 00:04:40,669
alternatives with its advice.

81
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:45,009
These displayed times are estimates, not
proof of a routed drive time.

82
00:04:45,229 --> 00:04:49,379
When testing is satisfactory, enable the
card and open New Call.

83
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:54,260
Enter TRAINING ONLY in the name and
nature, choose the matching type and

84
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:56,460
priority, and set the incident pin.

85
00:04:56,960 --> 00:05:00,080
Wait for the Run Card panel and resource
lists to refresh.

86
00:05:00,540 --> 00:05:04,300
Review the actual decision, advice,
selected resources,

87
00:05:04,509 --> 00:05:06,279
and any response code choices.

88
00:05:06,609 --> 00:05:09,429
The nearest list helps you inspect each
suggestion.

89
00:05:09,839 --> 00:05:14,130
You can change recipients before saving; a
highlighted suggestion is still a

90
00:05:14,130 --> 00:05:16,099
decision for the dispatcher to review.

91
00:05:16,310 --> 00:05:20,290
Save only inside your approved training
environment, then open

92
00:05:20,290 --> 00:05:23,589
the resulting call and confirm its
recipients and alarm level.

93
00:05:24,089 --> 00:05:27,649
A saved call does not establish that a
member received an alert;

94
00:05:27,969 --> 00:05:31,279
verify that separately through a
controlled communication test.

95
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:35,059
Further alarm levels add requirements to
an active response.

96
00:05:35,369 --> 00:05:38,209
Review those levels before using Strike
Next Alarm.

97
00:05:38,789 --> 00:05:42,459
When starting from a gallery template,
inspect its department mappings,

98
00:05:42,690 --> 00:05:46,360
keep the created card disabled, and repeat
the same saved-plan

99
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:48,370
test before putting it into use.

100
00:05:48,839 --> 00:05:51,570
The same planning controls fit other
organizations.

101
00:05:51,899 --> 00:05:55,019
A community paramedicine or mental health
team can request

102
00:05:55,019 --> 00:05:56,699
a clinician and peer responder.

103
00:05:57,010 --> 00:05:59,809
Search and rescue can request a search
team and leader.

104
00:06:00,129 --> 00:06:03,490
Security can combine a patrol unit with a
site supervisor.

105
00:06:03,839 --> 00:06:07,659
Wildland contractors can plan crew and
support resources, while an

106
00:06:07,659 --> 00:06:11,760
industrial team can request a response
unit and a hazardous-materials role.

107
00:06:12,179 --> 00:06:15,399
Configure the resource types,
qualification checks and local

108
00:06:15,399 --> 00:06:17,370
procedures for your own operation.

